ch = """
<h2 id="signed-spikes-memory-horizon">70. Signed spikes beat the transformer on two seeds, the interference failure turns out to be a memory that stopped remembering &mdash; and our screens were running at half speed all along</h2>
<p class="date">2026-09-29 &ndash; 09-30</p>

<p>We started from the open wound of <a href="#honest-gap-hybrid-levels-interference">&sect;69</a>: the full stack answered immediate questions perfectly and fell to chance with 64 tokens of text in between. The obvious suspect was the attention layer answering the synthetic questions and leaving the memory idle. <code>temp/diag_haystack_pathways.py</code> ablated each pathway and killed that idea. Switching attention off changed nothing, and switching the GDN memory off dropped every model to chance. <b>Recall is entirely the memory, in every model.</b> What differed was how long the memory held anything. On real text, the median decay half-life per memory channel was 47&ndash;76 tokens for plain <code>lif_exact</code>, but <b>0&ndash;1 token</b> for the hybrid, the full stack, Canon-A and the spiking MLP. With attention covering local context, the LM loss had simply taught the memory to wipe itself every step.</p>

<div class="milestone"><p><b>Signed multi-level spikes: 0.89&times; the transformer, recall restored, two seeds.</b> <code>lif_signed</code> on the full stack scored <b>61.36 / 61.11</b> (mean 61.24 vs the 2-layer transformer&rsquo;s 68.5). Recall held at 100&times;5, and the haystack came back to N8 88&ndash;90 / 63&ndash;65 / 34&ndash;35% (the full stack had 8/10/15). The memory half-life went back up to 97&ndash;113 / 55&ndash;62 tokens. Adding Canon-A made it <b>59.74</b> (1 seed), level with the <em>3-layer</em> transformer (59.38) using two layers. We do not know why signed spikes restore the horizon. The obvious hypothesis was tested and rejected: unsigned codes giving memory keys a shared direction. Key overlap was highest in <code>lif_exact</code>, the model with the <em>best</em> gapped recall.</p></div>

<p>Two follow-ups tempered it. A 3-layer signed <code>[spk, attn, spk]</code> scored 64.65 with its memory half-life back at ~2 tokens and the haystack at chance. The long horizon is a fragile learned equilibrium, not a property of signed spikes, which matters for the 9-layer v4. The gentler gap curriculum failed too. With gaps capped at 128, the MQAR aux never clicked in 100M tokens (total loss stuck near 9 against 4.3 without gaps). The 440-gap run of &sect;69 had only clicked after step 4000. So gaps delay recall acquisition; if we use them again, they should come after recall is learned.</p>

<p>Glenn asked whether a stateful model should be trained the way it lives: in a continuous stream instead of on random windows that start with amnesia. The June attempt (<a href="#stateful">&sect;10</a>) had been negative on a Hebbian memory. This time the case looked stronger. Carrying state at eval already gave random-trained models &minus;3 to &minus;7% ppl, and the 440-gap model got 24% <em>worse</em> with carried state. That is the &ldquo;unexplored states&rdquo; failure of Buitrago &amp; Gu (ICML 2025), for which state passing is the published fix. We scoped it (<code>docs/contiguous_training_design.md</code>), pre-registered the criteria, and fixed the stale June path (it had silently skipped the MQAR aux). We also added a SWAX-style stochastic attention window.</p>

<div class="setback"><p><b>Contiguous training: closed negative at 30M.</b> The two stateful runs matched each other (70.3 / 70.5 zero-state, streaming 65.3 / 65.2 vs the signed control&rsquo;s 64.3). They gained a little haystack at medium gaps and nothing at 440. Over one-million-token streams the signed random-trained models were already perfectly stable. Only the 440-gap model exploded (memory norm 27.7&rarr;75.5), exactly the case state passing exists for. The stochastic window changed nothing in training. But it showed that a <b>64-token attention window at inference costs only ~1% ppl</b> on signed models, so constant-memory inference is viable.</p></div>

<div class="milestone"><p><b>The screens had been running at half speed.</b> The stateful runs trained at 30&ndash;35k tok/s against 14&ndash;15k for everything else. Tracing it: the WSL virtual disk reports itself rotational with an 8 MB read-ahead. Every cold 2 KB random window read pulled 8 MB (~37 ms), and a 32-window batch took ~1.2 s, about the whole step. The drive is a SATA SSD, and sorting or threading the reads did not help. <code>MADV_RANDOM</code> on a separate memory map for the random-window path took a batch from 1220 ms to 28 ms with byte-identical data. Screens now run ~2&times; faster, and the v4 draft (72 random windows per step) should gain ~1.3&ndash;1.4&times;.</p></div>

<p>Two accounting corrections closed the session. The <code>[spike] spiking_only</code> log line rescaled a rate that <code>spike_rate_undiluted</code> had already made spiking-only, so every logged rate was doubled. Measured directly, signed spikes fire on <b>12.7%</b> of (neuron, token) slots, not 26% (lam 2.0: 6.7%; full stack 4.2%). <code>energy.py</code> had the same double count. The energy model itself then set expectations. At 512 tokens the LM head is 47% of per-token energy, the dense attention block 35%, and <b>spikes only 1%</b>, so sparsity alone cannot deliver a 90% energy claim. The real wins are elsewhere. Matching the 3-layer transformer&rsquo;s quality with 2 layers costs 1.6&times; less energy per token. With a 64-token window, per-token energy is constant in context length: 7.3&times; less than the quality-matched transformer at 32k tokens and 24&times; at 128k. Sparsity screens toward 10% and 5% are queued.</p>

<hr>
<p class="date">Major version: 1.55 (Haystack failure root-caused: recall = GDN memory in every model; hybrid/full/canon memory half-life 0-1 tok vs lif_exact 47-76. SIGNED spikes 61.36/61.11 = 0.89x tfmr, haystack + horizon restored; signed+canon 59.74 = 3L tfmr with 2 layers; signed 3L collapses [half-life ~2] -&gt; fragile equilibrium. gap128 failed [gaps delay MQAR acquisition]. Contiguous training CLOSED-NEG [r9a/b/c; 1M-token streams stable without it; only gap440 explodes]; SWA-64 inference ~1% cost. 30M screens were 2x data-bound [8 MB read-ahead] -&gt; MADV_RANDOM fix deployed. Spike log + energy.py double-counted: true firing 12.7%; spikes = 1% of energy; real energy wins = quality-matched 1.6x + long-context 7-24x with SWA. See docs/contiguous_training_design.md + neuro_ppl_gap_diagnosis_2026_09_27 + HANDOFF_2026_09_30.)</p>
"""
